{"hash":"23aeb3f2aeab83ae","voice":"en-IN-NeerjaExpressiveNeural","rate":"+4%","dur":113.109,"said":"Somewhere below the temple ahead of you is a small cave, and in it begins one of the great stories of Indian thought. More than twelve hundred years ago, a boy left his home at Kaladi, in Kerala, far in the south, determined to become a sannyasi. He walked north, a very long way, until he reached the banks of the Narmada, here at Omkareshwar. In a cave on this island sat an old sage, Govinda Bhagavatpada, deep in meditation. The boy bowed and asked to be taught, and the sage accepted him. Here Shankara studied at his guru’s feet. Then, the old biographies say, the rains came. The Narmada rose in flood and came roaring toward the cave where Govinda sat absorbed in samadhi. The young disciple did not run. He set his kamandalu, his little water pot, in the path of the flood, and the raging river was drawn into it, and the guru was safe. When his learning was complete, his guru sent him out into the world, and that boy became Adi Shankaracharya, the great teacher of Advaita, the oneness of all that is. Now lift your eyes to the top of Mandhata hill. On 21 September 2023 a statue of him was unveiled there, 108 feet tall, in bronze, standing on a lotus of stone. It shows him not as an old master but as a boy of twelve, as he is remembered here. Its name says what he taught: the Statue of Oneness.\n\nDid you know? The statue weighs about 100 tonnes and stands on a 75-foot pedestal. Its bronze is 88% copper, 4% zinc and 8% tin, and it was made under the guidance of sculptor Bhagwan Rampure and painter Vasudev Kamath.","segments":[{"key":"text","start":0,"end":1311},{"key":"didYouKnow","start":1327,"end":1532}],"words":[[0.098,0.517,0,9],[0.627,0.312,10,15],[0.951,0.12,16,19],[1.084,0.409,20,26],[1.504,0.204,27,32],[1.721,0.168,33,35],[1.901,0.264,36,39],[2.177,0.132,40,42],[2.322,0.06,43,44],[2.394,0.385,45,50],[2.79,0.397,51,55],[3.355,0.18,57,60],[3.548,0.072,61,63],[3.632